</aside><button class="sidebar-toggle" aria-label="Toggle sidebar">☰</button><main class="main"><div class="container"><section class="hero"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="index.html">Associate</a><span class="sep">›</span><a href="#">Module 3</a><span class="sep">›</span><span>Filtering &amp; Sorting</span></div><span class="hero-eyebrow">Module 3 · Lesson 5</span><h1>Filtering, Sorting, and Data Selection</h1><p class="hero-sub">Master the essential techniques for filtering rows, selecting columns, sorting data, and extracting subsets using boolean masks, .loc, .iloc, and query methods. These skills are fundamental to data exploration and manipulation in professional data science workflows.</p></section><div class="objectives"><h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Understand and apply boolean indexing with numpy arrays and pandas Series</li><li>Use .loc and .iloc for label-based and position-based selection</li><li>Master the query() method for readable, complex filtering</li><li>Sort data efficiently using sort_values() with multiple parameters</li><li>Apply nlargest() and nsmallest() for top-k value selection</li><li>Build efficient data pipelines combining filtering and selection</li></ul></div><section class="section" id="boolean-indexing"><span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span><h2>Boolean Indexing and Masks</h2><p>Boolean indexing is the cornerstone of data filtering. Rather than manually iterating through rows, we create boolean arrays (True/False) that indicate which elements to keep. This approach is both elegant and computationally efficient, leveraging NumPy's vectorized operations.</p><p>Think of a boolean mask like a sieve: each True value lets data through, while each False blocks it. When you pass this sieve to your DataFrame, you get only the rows where the sieve has holes.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">pandas</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">pd</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Create sample DataFrame of employee data</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'name'</span>: [<span class="st">'Alice'</span>, <span class="st">'Bob'</span>, <span class="st">'Charlie'</span>, <span class="st">'Diana'</span>, <span class="st">'Eve'</span>],
    <span class="st">'salary'</span>: [<span class="nm">75000</span>, <span class="nm">65000</span>, <span class="nm">95000</span>, <span class="nm">80000</span>, <span class="nm">72000</span>],
    <span class="st">'department'</span>: [<span class="st">'Engineering'</span>, <span class="st">'Sales'</span>, <span class="st">'Engineering'</span>, <span class="st">'Marketing'</span>, <span class="st">'Sales'</span>]
})

<span class="cm"># Create boolean mask for high earners (salary > 75000)</span>
<span class="va">high_earners</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'salary'</span>] <span class="op">></span> <span class="nm">75000</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">high_earners</span>)  <span class="cm"># Shows: [False, False, True, True, False]</span>

<span class="cm"># Apply mask to get filtered DataFrame</span>
<span class="va">filtered_df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="va">high_earners</span>]
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">filtered_df</span>)</code></pre>
<div class="key-concept"><strong>Key Concept:</strong> Combining multiple conditions with boolean operators: & (AND), | (OR), ~ (NOT). Use parentheses around each condition to ensure proper precedence.</div><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># Multiple conditions combined</span>
<span class="va">engineers_high_pay</span> <span class="op">=</span> (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'department'</span>] <span class="op">==</span> <span class="st">'Engineering'</span>) <span class="op">&</span> (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'salary'</span>] <span class="op">></span> <span class="nm">80000</span>)
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="va">engineers_high_pay</span>]

<span class="cm"># Using OR to find either condition</span>
<span class="va">marketing_or_sales</span> <span class="op">=</span> (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'department'</span>] <span class="op">==</span> <span class="st">'Marketing'</span>) <span class="op">|</span> (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'department'</span>] <span class="op">==</span> <span class="st">'Sales'</span>)
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="va">marketing_or_sales</span>]

<span class="cm"># Using NOT to exclude values</span>
<span class="va">not_engineering</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="op">~</span>(<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'department'</span>] <span class="op">==</span> <span class="st">'Engineering'</span>)
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="va">not_engineering</span>]</code></pre>
<div class="callout-box tip"><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Boolean indexing creates a view in some cases and a copy in others. When you need explicit control, use .copy() to ensure you have a copy: df[mask].copy().</div></section><section class="section" id="loc-iloc"><span class="section-label">Selection Methods</span><h2>Label-Based and Position-Based Selection</h2><p>.loc (location) and .iloc (integer location) are powerful tools for flexible data selection. .loc works with labels (row names, column names), while .iloc works with integer positions (like list indexing).</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># Create DataFrame with labeled index</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'product'</span>: [<span class="st">'Laptop'</span>, <span class="st">'Mouse'</span>, <span class="st">'Keyboard'</span>, <span class="st">'Monitor'</span>],
    <span class="st">'price'</span>: [<span class="nm">1200</span>, <span class="nm">25</span>, <span class="nm">75</span>, <span class="nm">300</span>],
    <span class="st">'stock'</span>: [<span class="nm">15</span>, <span class="nm">200</span>, <span class="nm">150</span>, <span class="nm">45</span>]
}, index=[<span class="st">'A'</span>, <span class="st">'B'</span>, <span class="st">'C'</span>, <span class="st">'D'</span>])

<span class="cm"># .loc uses labels - select row 'B' and specific columns</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">loc</span>[<span class="st">'B'</span>, [<span class="st">'product'</span>, <span class="st">'price'</span>]]

<span class="cm"># .iloc uses positions - select 2nd row, columns 0-1</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">iloc</span>[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>:<span class="nm">2</span>]

<span class="cm"># .loc with boolean array</span>
<span class="va">mask</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'stock'</span>] <span class="op">></span> <span class="nm">100</span>
<span class="va">high_stock</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">loc</span>[<span class="va">mask</span>, <span class="st">'product'</span>]

<span class="cm"># .iloc with slices - rows 1-3, all columns</span>
<span class="va">subset</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">iloc</span>[<span class="nm">1</span>:<span class="nm">3</span>, :]</code></pre>
<div class="compare-grid">
<div class="compare-item">
<div class="compare-title">.loc (Label-Based)</div>
<div class="compare-content">Works with row/column labels<br>Inclusive of end points: [0:5] gets 0-5<br>Raises KeyError if label missing<br>Better for named indices</div>
</div>
<div class="compare-item">
<div class="compare-title">.iloc (Position-Based)</div>
<div class="compare-content">Works with integer positions<br>Exclusive of end points: [0:5] gets 0-4<br>Raises IndexError if position invalid<br>Better for unknown labels</div>
</div>
</div></section><section class="section" id="query"><span class="section-label">Advanced Filtering</span><h2>The query() Method</h2><p>The query() method provides a more readable syntax for complex filtering operations, especially when dealing with multiple conditions. It accepts string expressions that are evaluated against DataFrame columns, making your intent crystal clear.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># Complex filter using boolean indexing (hard to read)</span>
<span class="va">result1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[(<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'salary'</span>] <span class="op">></span> <span class="nm">70000</span>) <span class="op">&</span> (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'department'</span>] <span class="op">==</span> <span class="st">'Engineering'</span>) <span class="op">&</span> (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'experience'</span>] <span class="op">></span> <span class="nm">3</span>)]

<span class="cm"># Same filter using query() (much more readable!)</span>
<span class="va">result2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">query</span>(<span class="st">'salary > 70000 and department == "Engineering" and experience > 3'</span>)

<span class="cm"># query() with variables using @</span>
<span class="va">min_salary</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">75000</span>
<span class="va">dept</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">'Engineering'</span>
<span class="va">filtered</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">query</span>(<span class="st">'salary > @min_salary and department == @dept'</span>)</code></pre>
<div class="callout-box warning"><strong>Important:</strong> query() is excellent for readability but has slight performance overhead. For simple, single-column filters, boolean indexing might be faster. Profile your code to determine when query() is worth the clarity.</div></section><section class="section" id="sorting"><span class="section-label">Ordering Data</span><h2>Sorting with sort_values()</h2><p>Sorting is crucial for exploratory data analysis and identifying patterns. The sort_values() method provides flexible control over sort order, handling of missing values, and multi-column sorting.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># Basic sorting ascending (default)</span>
<span class="va">sorted_by_salary</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">sort_values</span>(<span class="st">'salary'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Sort descending</span>
<span class="va">highest_paid</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">sort_values</span>(<span class="st">'salary'</span>, <span class="va">ascending</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">False</span>)

<span class="cm"># Sort by multiple columns</span>
<span class="va">sorted_dept_salary</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">sort_values</span>(
    [<span class="st">'department'</span>, <span class="st">'salary'</span>],
    <span class="va">ascending</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="kw">True</span>, <span class="kw">False</span>]  <span class="cm"># Sort dept A-Z, then salary high-low</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Handle missing values in sorting</span>
<span class="va">sorted_with_na</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">sort_values</span>(<span class="st">'bonus'</span>, <span class="va">na_position</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'last'</span>)  <span class="cm"># Put NaNs at end</span>

<span class="cm"># In-place sorting (modifies original DataFrame)</span>
<span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">sort_values</span>(<span class="st">'salary'</span>, <span class="va">inplace</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">True</span>)</code></pre></section><section class="section" id="top-k"><span class="section-label">Top-K Selection</span><h2>Finding Top and Bottom Values</h2><p>Rather than sorting the entire DataFrame and slicing, use nlargest() and nsmallest() to efficiently extract the top-k rows. These methods are optimized and much faster for large datasets when you only need a small subset.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># Get top 5 highest paid employees</span>
<span class="va">top_earners</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">nlargest</span>(<span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="st">'salary'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Get bottom 3 paid employees</span>
<span class="va">lowest_earners</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">nsmallest</span>(<span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="st">'salary'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Get top 10 products by stock, only specific columns</span>
<span class="va">top_stock</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">nlargest</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="st">'stock'</span>)[[<span class="st">'product'</span>, <span class="st">'stock'</span>]]

<span class="cm"># Efficiency comparison</span>
<span class="cm"># FAST: df.nlargest(5, 'salary')</span>
<span class="cm"># SLOWER: df.sort_values('salary', ascending=False).head(5)</span></code></pre>
<div class="term-grid">
<div class="term-item">
<div class="term-name">nlargest(n, columns)</div>
<div class="term-desc">Returns n rows with largest values in specified column(s). Optimized for partial sorting.</div>
</div>
<div class="term-item">
<div class="term-name">nsmallest(n, columns)</div>
<div class="term-desc">Returns n rows with smallest values in specified column(s). Inverse of nlargest.</div>
</div>
<div class="term-item">
<div class="term-name">isin(values)</div>
<div class="term-desc">Filters for rows where column values are in the provided list. Useful for categorical filtering.</div>
</div>
<div class="term-item">
<div class="term-name">between(left, right)</div>
<div class="term-desc">Filters for rows where values fall within a range (inclusive). Clean alternative to compound conditions.</div>
</div>
</div></section><section class="section" id="practice"><span class="section-label">Hands-On Practice</span><h2>Building a Data Analysis Pipeline</h2><p>Let's combine all these techniques into a real-world scenario: analyzing sales data to identify high-value opportunities.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># Create realistic sales dataset</span>
<span class="va">sales_df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'date'</span>: <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">date_range</span>(<span class="st">'2024-01-01'</span>, <span class="va">periods</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1000</span>),
    <span class="st">'customer'</span>: <span class="im">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">choice</span>([<span class="st">'Acme'</span>, <span class="st">'TechCorp'</span>, <span class="st">'Global Inc'</span>], <span class="nm">1000</span>),
    <span class="st">'amount'</span>: <span class="im">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randint</span>(<span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="nm">5000</span>, <span class="nm">1000</span>),
    <span class="st">'region'</span>: <span class="im">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">choice</span>([<span class="st">'North'</span>, <span class="st">'South'</span>, <span class="st">'East'</span>, <span class="st">'West'</span>], <span class="nm">1000</span>)
})

<span class="cm"># Pipeline: Find high-value deals for targeted follow-up</span>
<span class="va">high_value_deals</span> <span class="op">=</span> (
    <span class="va">sales_df</span>
    .<span class="fn">query</span>(<span class="st">'amount > 2000 and region != "South"'</span>)
    .<span class="fn">sort_values</span>(<span class="st">'amount'</span>, <span class="va">ascending</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">False</span>)
    .<span class="fn">head</span>(<span class="nm">20</span>)
)

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">high_value_deals</span>)</code></pre><div class="callout-box tip"><strong>Best Practice:</strong> Chain operations together using method chaining for cleaner, more readable code. Each method returns a DataFrame, allowing the next method to operate on it.</div><h3>Exercises</h3><ol><li>Filter the sales_df to show only transactions from 'TechCorp' with amounts greater than 1500, sorted by date descending.</li><li>Use nlargest() to find the 10 largest transactions and display only the date and amount columns.</li><li>Create a boolean mask for transactions in either 'North' or 'East' region with amounts between 500 and 3000.</li><li>Using query(), find all 'Acme' transactions after March 1st, 2024 with amount > 1000.</li></ol></section><section class="section" id="summary"><span class="section-label">Summary</span><h2>Key Takeaways</h2><ul><li><strong>Boolean Indexing:</strong> Create True/False masks with comparison operators, combine with &, |, ~. This is the foundational technique for data filtering.</li><li><strong>.loc vs .iloc:</strong> Use .loc for label-based selection, .iloc for position-based. Both support single values, slices, lists, and boolean arrays.</li><li><strong>query() Method:</strong> Provides SQL-like readability for complex filters. Use @ to reference external variables.</li><li><strong>sort_values():</strong> Master multi-column sorting with ascending parameter. Handle NaNs with na_position parameter.</li><li><strong>Top-K Selection:</strong> Use nlargest() and nsmallest() instead of sorting for partial data extraction—it's faster and more intent-revealing.</li><li><strong>Method Chaining:</strong> Combine operations elegantly by chaining methods together, building readable data pipelines.</li></ul></section></div><div class="nav-buttons"><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module3-lesson4" class="nav-btn prev"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M15 18l-6-6 6-6"/></svg> Previous</a><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module3-lesson6" class="nav-btn next">Next <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></div></div></main><footer><div class="container"><div class="footer-logo"><img src="../../assets/logo_only.png" alt="SustainSys" class="footer-logo-img"><div class="footer-logo-text">Sustain<span>Sys</span> AI Academy</div></div><p>© 2026 SustainSys. All rights reserved.</p><p><a href="https://sustainsys.co.uk">sustainsys.co.uk</a></p></div></footer><script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@supabase/supabase-js@2/dist/umd/supabase.min.js"></script>
